  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThis morning's upper air maps show little change from this time \nyesterday outside of a slight eastward progression of the upper-\nlevel ridge, turning flow aloft slightly more westerly today. \nTemperatures stayed mild overnight, with the majority of the urban \ncorridor starting today out in the mid to upper 60s. This should \nmake for another hot day across the forecast area, as temperatures \nare expected to warm into the low to mid 90s before we see scattered \nshowers and storms develop this afternoon. A mountain wave cloud is \nobserved across the northern urban corridor this morning that may\nslow surface heating beneath it, but slightly cooling 700 mb \ntemperatures this afternoon associated with a mid-level front, \nwill aid in increased instability and help kick off showers and \nstorms by this afternoon. CAMs continue to show slightly better \nstorm coverage today, with a round of more diurnal convection \nexpected this afternoon ahead of a more linear cluster this \nevening. Today's CAMs also show a more predominant surface front \nor outflow moving from northwest to southeast across the forecast \narea with this evening's showers/storms, bringing a swath of \nstrong to severe winds across the urban corridor (50-60 mph) as it\npasses, with speeds gradually diminishing as they move across the\nplains. Showers may linger through late tonight before things \nstabilize. \n\nOn Sunday, an upper-level low is expected to move across the \nNorthern Rockies, shifting the center of the upper-level ridge east \nover Oklahoma/Arkansas. This will allow for mid-level monsoonal \nmoisture to continue to flow into Colorado. PWATs are expected to\naverage around 130% of normal, and afternoon showers and storms \nare expected once again. MLCAPE looks to slightly increase over \ntoday, and this morning's CAMs show some strong to severe winds \npossible along a line of storms across the plains. 0-6 km bulk \nshear will range from 25-35 kts that will support potential for a \nfew more organized storms. Afternoon highs are expected to be a \nfew degrees cooler behind today's front, with portions of the \nurban corridor staying under 90F for a change.\n\nOne more active day is expected on Monday as PWAT values are\nexpected to increase a tad more (150-170% of normal). Despite the\nincreased moisture, southwesterly flow aloft will increase and \nkeep afternoon showers and storms moving swiftly, helping to limit\nflooding concerns, though there may be some localized heavy\nrainfall along the Front Range Mountains, where moisture is\nexpected to be greatest. Some uncertainties remain with regards \nto the proximity of a 500 mb jet max throughout the day, that may \ndetermine the best location for storm development. Additionally, \ntiming of an expected shortwave could enhance synoptic forcings if\nit moves through in the afternoon. The latest GFS would support \nat least some slight benefits with it showing it passing over \nColorado in the late afternoon/early evening. However, the ECMWF \nshows a delayed arrival, with it passing through closer to Tuesday\nmorning. Will have to continue to monitor these subtle\ndifferences, but expect greater coverage of storms to be in the\narea of the right entrance of the speed max. Temperatures are\nforecast to be near normal, with low to mid 80s expected across\nthe plains, and low to mid 70s for the mountain valleys. \n\nA gradual warming and drying trend is expected from Tuesday \nonward with southwesterly flow aloft persisting as the upper- \nlevel ridge remains centered over the eastern CONUS. There will \nstill be enough moisture in place for some isolated to scattered \nafternoon showers/storms each afternoon.",
